#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b54258-61d2-549c-94e4-a251f6d2a656" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19f5f01-948e-5f44-8016-63073ed31dbc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Function Multiple Rule for Differentiation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115392 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a146d5d3-2562-5b8b-886e-4cf6519d3282" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Chain Rule for Differentiation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115393 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54a6dbe-42cb-5614-ae0b-611de1719b45" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Product Rules for Differentiation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116440 :tutorial/content "Many of the known properties of derivatives of scalar functions extend naturally to derivatives of vector functions. \n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are vector-valued functions, $u(t)$ is a scalar function, and $\\alpha$ and $\\mathbf c$ are scalar and vector constants, respectively. \n\nThe **sum and multiple rules** for differentiating vector-valued functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)^{'}(t)={f}^{'}(t)+{g}^{'}(t)\\, & \\text{sum rule} \\\\ (\\alpha f)^{'}(t)=\\alpha {f}^{'}(t),\\,(uc)^{'}(t)={u}^{'}(t)c\\, & \\text{constant multiple rules} \\\\ (uf)^{'}(t)={u}^{'}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{'}(t)\\, & \\text{scalar function multiple rule}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "10b4a11f-6ada-568e-9a43-dbeeeb1826c6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2143 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116441 :tutorial/content "The **chain rule** for differentiating vector-valued functions states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c}( \\mathbf{f} ( u ( t ) ) {)}^{\\prime } = {u}^{\\prime } ( t ) {\\mathbf{f}}^{\\prime } ( u ( t ) ) ,\\end{array}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{f}(u)$ is a vector-valued function while $u(t)$ is a scalar function." :tutorial/id #uuid "e299896a-b9ca-574f-8c05-ef656d9c5cd2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2144 :tutorial/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116442 :tutorial/content "When working with vector functions, there are two types of products: the dot product and the cross product. So, there are two **product rules** for derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\cdot g)^{'}(t)=f(t)\\cdot {g}^{'}(t)+{f}^{'}(t)\\cdot g(t)\\, & \\text{dot product rule} \\\\ (f\\times g)^{'}(t)={f}^{'}(t)\\times g(t)+f(t)\\times {g}^{'}(t)\\, & \\text{cross product rule}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Remember that the cross product is anticommutative, meaning that $\\mathbf f \\times \\mathbf g = - \\mathbf g \\times \\mathbf f.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\times \\mathbf g)(t) = -\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf g \\times \\mathbf f)(t).\n$$\n\nWhen applying the cross product rule, the order in which the operations are applied is important." :tutorial/id #uuid "babb1734-198a-5751-b78c-b333b6e38d5e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2145 :tutorial/title "The Product Rules for Differentiation"}]}
